.TH tkupath n 0.3 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkupath
.PP
A breadcrumb path bar. Shows a path as a row of clickable segments: \fB/home/greg/docs\fR becomes \fB[/] [home] [greg] [docs]\fR. Clicking a segment fires a callback with the path up to and including that segment, so a file manager can navigate to any ancestor in one click. Tk 8.6+ and 9.x.
.SS API
.CS
::tkutils::tkupath::widget  path ?options?
::tkutils::tkupath::setPath path p     ;# show p as segments
::tkutils::tkupath::getPath path       ;# the currently shown path
.CE
.SS Options
.IP \(bu
\fB\-onnavigate\fR script, appended the clicked path \-\- run when a segment is clicked.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-rootlabel\fR  label shown for the root segment (default \fB/\fR).
.SS Use
.CS
package require tkutils::tkupath

::tkutils::tkupath::widget .bar \
    -onnavigate {apply {p { puts "go $p" }}}
pack .bar -fill x

::tkutils::tkupath::setPath .bar /home/greg/docs
.CE
.PP
Clicking \fBgreg\fR shows \fB/home/greg\fR in the bar and then fires the callback with \fB/home/greg\fR; the caller decides what navigation means (change a directory listing, re\-root a tree). The bar has already updated itself, so the callback does not need \fBsetPath\fR \- only a navigation that ends somewhere else (a redirect, a failed change) calls \fBsetPath\fR with the real location.
.SS "Windows paths"
.PP
A drive path shows the drive as the first segment, a UNC path the share; backslashes count as \fB/\fR. The targets are real paths:
.TS
l l l.
path	segments	targets
\fBS:/gregor/daten\fR	\fBS:\fR \fBgregor\fR \fBdaten\fR	\fBS:/\fR \fBS:/gregor\fR \fBS:/gregor/daten\fR
\fB//srv/share/x\fR	\fB//srv/share\fR \fBx\fR	\fB//srv/share/\fR \fB//srv/share/x\fR
.TE
.PP
Up to 0.2 the root \fB/\fR was put in front of every target (\fB/S:/gregor\fR), which Windows reads as a path on the current drive. Since 0.3.
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBtkufiletree\fR, \fBtkufilelist\fR, \fBtkutab\fR
